o bricklayers, these were particularly associated because they often work outdoors. Compare Maurerdekolleté.",{"g":"f","decl":"||||n|n|n|n"}]],"senses":[[723222,"maurerbrause","noun","synonym of Bier (“beer”)",0,"f",["informal","humorous"]]]},"meteorologischem":{"entries":[["meteorologischem",null,null,null]],"senses":[[359442,"meteorologischem","adj","strong dative masculine/neuter singular of meteorologisch",0]],"inflections":[["meteorologischem","meteorologisch","dative,masculine,singular,strong,without-article"]]},"seehund":{"entries":[["seehund",null,"[ˈzeːˌhʊnt]","15th century, alteration of Seelhund, explanatory compound from now obsolete Seel (“seal”) + Hund (“dog”). The first component seems to have been inherited from Middle High German sële, sëleh, from Old High German sëlah, from Proto-Germanic *selhaz (“seal”). For natural reasons it was a rare word, however, and was later reinforced by cognate Middle Low German sēl, also sēlhunt. Cognate with English seal, North Frisian selich, seel, Danish sæl. Ultimately reinterpreted as See (“sea”) + Hund (“dog”); compare the same in Dutch zeehond.",{"g":"m","decl":"||/e|s/es|3ünde|3ünde|3ünden|3ünde"}]],"senses":[[24457,"seehund","noun","common seal, harbor seal (Phoca vitulina)",0,"m"],[24458,"seehund","noun","any seal",1,"m"]],"related_forms":[[34962,"seehund","seelöwe","related"],[34963,"seehund","walross","related"]]},"taugte":{"entries":[["taugte",null,null,null]],"senses":[[139430,"taugte","verb","inflection of taugen:",0],[139431,"taugte","verb","first/third-person singular preterite",1],[139432,"taugte","verb","first/third-person singular subjunctive II",2]],"inflections":[["taugte","taugen","past"]]},"vorangehende":{"entries":[["vorangehende",null,null,null]],"senses":[[203674,"vorangehende","adj","inflection of vorangehend:",0],[203675,"vorangehende","adj","strong/mixed nominative/accusative feminine singular",1],[203676,"vorangehende","adj","strong nominative/accusative plural",2],[203677,"vorangehende",